          %h2#idea Idea
          %p
            The
            %strong electromagnetic field
            is is a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/gauge+field"} gauge
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/field+%28physics%29"} field
            which unifies the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/electric+field"} electric field
            and the
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/magnetic+field"} magnetic field
            %strong electromagnetic field
            on a space
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            in the
            %em absence
            of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/magnetic+charge"} magnetic charge
            is modeled by a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cocycle"} cocycle
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mover
                    %mi F
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ^
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \hat F
            in degree 2
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/ordinary+differential+cohomology"} ordinary differential cohomology
          %p This may be realized in particular equivalently by
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                a
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/principal+U%281%29-bundle"} principal U(1)-bundle
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/connection+on+a+bundle"} with connection
                on
                = succeed ";" do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi X
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            %li
              %p
                a degree 2
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Deligne+cohomology"} Deligne cocycle
                on
                = succeed "." do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi X
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
          %p
            In the presence of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/magnetic+charge"} magnetic charge
            the electromagnetic field is modeled by a cocycle in differential
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/twisted+cohomology"} twisted cohomology
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/magnetic+current"} magnetic current
          %p
            The analogous field modeled by a degree 3
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Deligne+cohomology"} Deligne cocycle
            is the
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Kalb-Ramond+field"} Kalb-Ramond field
          %h2#history History
          %p …historical section eventually goes here..
          %p
            …electricity and magnetism were discovered independently,
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Maxwell%27s+equations"} Maxwell's equations
            in classical vector analysis which allows the formulation as a tensor
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi F
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F
            as below, and “magnetism is a consequence of electrostatics and covariance, hence the composite noun electromagnetism”…
          %p (…)
          %h2#mathematical_model_from_physical_input Mathematical model from physical input
          %p
            The electromagnetic field is modeled by a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/circle+bundle"} circle bundle
            with
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/connection+on+a+bundle"} connection
          %p We describe how this identification arises from experimental input.
          %p The input is two-fold
          %ol
            %li
              %p
                %a{:href => "#MaxwellEquations"} Maxwell’s equations
                say that (using the experimentally observed absence of net
                = succeed ")" do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/magnetic+charge"} magnetic charge
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/field+strength"} field strength
                of electromagnetism is a closed
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/differential+form"} differential 2-form
                on
                = succeed ";" do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/spacetime"} spacetime
            %li
              %p
                The
                %em Dirac charge quantization argument
                shows that in order for the electromagnetic field to serve as the
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/background+gauge+field"} background gauge field
                to which a charged
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+mechanics"} quantum mechanical
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/particle"} particle
                couples (for instance an
                = succeed ")," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/electron"} electron
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/curvature"} curvature
                2-form of a
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/circle+bundle"} circle bundle
                with
                = succeed "." do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/connection+on+a+bundle"} connection
          %p We say this now in more detail.
          %h3#MaxwellEquations Maxwell’s equations
          %p
            We first discuss in
            %a{:href => "#FieldStrengthAsClosed2Form"} Field strength as a closed 2-form
            how
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Maxwell%27s+equations"} Maxwell's equations
            state that the electromagnetic
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/field+strength"} field strength
            is a closed
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/differential+form"} differential 2-form
            on
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/spacetime"} spacetime
          %h4#FieldStrengthAsClosed2Form Field strength is a closed 2-form
          %p
            In modern language, the insight of (
            %a{:href => "#Maxwell"}> Maxwell, 1865
            ) is that locally, when physical
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/spacetime"} spacetime
            is well approximated by a patch of its tangent space, i.e. by a patch of 4-dimensional
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Minkowski+space"} Minkowski space
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi U
                    %mo ⊂
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %msup
                      %mi ℝ
                      %mn 4
                    %mo ,
                    %mi g
                    %mo =
                    %mi diag
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                    %mn 1
                    %mo ,
                    %mn 1
                    %mo ,
                    %mn 1
                    %mo ,
                    %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} U \subset (\mathbb{R}^4, g = diag(-1,1,1,1))
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mover
                    %mi E
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} →
                  %mo =
                  %mrow
                    %mo [
                    %mrow
                      %mtable
                        %mtr
                          %mtd
                            %msub
                              %mi E
                              %mn 1
                        %mtr
                          %mtd
                            %msub
                              %mi E
                              %mn 2
                        %mtr
                          %mtd
                            %msub
                              %mi E
                              %mn 3
                    %mo ]
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \vec E = \left[ \array{E_1 \\ E_2 \\ E_3} \right]
            and magnetic field
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mover
                    %mi B
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} →
                  %mo =
                  %mrow
                    %mo [
                    %mrow
                      %mtable
                        %mtr
                          %mtd
                            %msub
                              %mi B
                              %mn 1
                        %mtr
                          %mtd
                            %msub
                              %mi B
                              %mn 2
                        %mtr
                          %mtd
                            %msub
                              %mi B
                              %mn 3
                    %mo ]
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \vec B = \left[ \array{B_1 \\ B_2 \\ B_3} \right]
            combine into a differential
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/differential+form"} 2-form
            – the
            %strong Faraday tensor
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mrow
                    %mtable{:columnalign => "right left right left right left right left right left", :columnspacing => "0em", :displaystyle => "true"}
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %mi F
                        %mtd
                          %mo ≔
                          %mi E
                          %mo ∧
                          %mi d
                          %mi t
                          %mo +
                          %mi B
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                          %mo ≔
                          %msub
                            %mi E
                            %mn 1
                          %mi d
                          %msup
                            %mi x
                            %mn 1
                          %mo ∧
                          %mi d
                          %mi t
                          %mo +
                          %msub
                            %mi E
                            %mn 2
                          %mi d
                          %msup
                            %mi x
                            %mn 2
                          %mo ∧
                          %mi d
                          %mi t
                          %mo +
                          %msub
                            %mi E
                            %mn 3
                          %mi d
                          %msup
                            %mi x
                            %mn 3
                          %mo ∧
                          %mi d
                          %mi t
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                          %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} +
                          %msub
                            %mi B
                            %mn 1
                          %mi d
                          %msup
                            %mi x
                            %mn 2
                          %mo ∧
                          %mi d
                          %msup
                            %mi x
                            %mn 3
                          %mo +
                          %msub
                            %mi B
                            %mn 2
                          %mi d
                          %msup
                            %mi x
                            %mn 3
                          %mo ∧
                          %mi d
                          %msup
                            %mi x
                            %mn 1
                          %mo +
                          %msub
                            %mi B
                            %mn 3
                          %mi d
                          %msup
                            %mi x
                            %mn 1
                          %mo ∧
                          %mi d
                          %msup
                            %mi x
                            %mn 2
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\begin{aligned}
                  F &amp; \coloneqq E \wedge d t + B
                  \\\
                  \&amp;
                  \\coloneqq
                  E_1 d x^1 \wedge d t +
                  E_2 d x^2 \wedge d t +
                  E_3 d x^3 \wedge d t
                  \\\
                  \&amp; +
                  B_1 d x^2 \wedge d x^3 +
                  B_2 d x^3 \wedge d x^1 +
                  B_3 d x^1 \wedge d x^2
                  \\end{aligned}
          %p
            in
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi Ω
                    %mn 2
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi U
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Omega^2(U)
            and the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/electric+charge"} electric charge
            density and current density combine to a differential 3-form
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mrow
                    %mtable{:columnalign => "right left right left right left right left right left", :columnspacing => "0em", :displaystyle => "true"}
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %msub
                            %mi j
                            %mi el
                        %mtd
                          %mo ≔
                          %mi j
                          %mo ∧
                          %mi dt
                          %mo −
                          %mi ρ
                          %mi d
                          %msup
                            %mi x
                            %mn 1
                          %mo ∧
                          %mi d
                          %msup
                            %mi x
                            %mn 2
                          %mo ∧
                          %mi d
                          %msup
                            %mi x
                            %mn 3
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                          %mo ≔
                          %msub
                            %mi j
                            %mn 1
                          %mi d
                          %msup
                            %mi x
                            %mn 2
                          %mo ∧
                          %mi d
                          %msup
                            %mi x
                            %mn 3
                          %mo ∧
                          %mi d
                          %mi t
                          %mo +
                          %msub
                            %mi j
                            %mn 2
                          %mi d
                          %msup
                            %mi x
                            %mn 3
                          %mo ∧
                          %mi d
                          %msup
                            %mi x
                            %mn 1
                          %mo ∧
                          %mi d
                          %mi t
                          %mo +
                          %msub
                            %mi j
                            %mn 3
                          %mi d
                          %msup
                            %mi x
                            %mn 1
                          %mo ∧
                          %mi d
                          %msup
                            %mi x
                            %mn 2
                          %mo ∧
                          %mi d
                          %mi t
                          %mo −
                          %mi ρ
                          %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                          %mi d
                          %msup
                            %mi x
                            %mn 1
                          %mo ∧
                          %mi d
                          %msup
                            %mi x
                            %mn 2
                          %mo ∧
                          %mi d
                          %msup
                            %mi x
                            %mn 3
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\begin{aligned}
                  j_{el} &amp; \coloneqq j\wedge dt - \rho d x^1 \wedge d x^2 \wedge d x^3 \\
                  \&amp; \coloneqq
                  j_1 d x^2 \wedge d x^3 \wedge d t +
                  j_2 d x^3 \wedge d x^1 \wedge d t +
                  j_3 d x^1 \wedge d x^2 \wedge d t
                  \-
                  \\rho \; d x^1 \wedge d x^2 \wedge d x^3
                  \\end{aligned}
          %p
            in
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi Ω
                    %mn 3
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi U
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Omega^3(U)
            such that the following two equations of differential forms are satisfied
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mrow
                    %mtable{:columnalign => "right left right left right left right left right left", :columnspacing => "0em", :displaystyle => "true"}
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %mi d
                          %mi F
                          %mo =
                          %mn 0
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %mi d
                          %mo ⋆
                          %mi F
                          %mo =
                          %msub
                            %mi j
                            %mi el
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo ,
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\begin{aligned}
                  d F = 0
                  \\\
                  d \star F  = j_{el}
                  \\end{aligned}
                  \\,,
          %p
            where
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi d
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} d
            is the de Rham differential operator and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo ⋆
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \star
            the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Hodge+star"} Hodge star
            operator. If we decompose
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo ⋆
                  %mi F
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \star F
            into its components as before as
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mrow
                    %mtable{:columnalign => "right left right left right left right left right left", :columnspacing => "0em", :displaystyle => "true"}
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %mo ⋆
                          %mi F
                        %mtd
                          %mo =
                          %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                          %mi D
                          %mo +
                          %mi H
                          %mo ∧
                          %mi dt
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                          %mo =
                          %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                          %msub
                            %mi D
                            %mn 1
                          %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                          %mi d
                          %msup
                            %mi x
                            %mn 2
                          %mo ∧
                          %mi d
                          %msup
                            %mi x
                            %mn 3
                          %mo −
                          %msub
                            %mi D
                            %mn 2
                          %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                          %mi d
                          %msup
                            %mi x
                            %mn 3
                          %mo ∧
                          %mi d
                          %msup
                            %mi x
                            %mn 1
                          %mo −
                          %msub
                            %mi D
                            %mn 3
                          %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                          %mi d
                          %msup
                            %mi x
                            %mn 1
                          %mo ∧
                          %mi d
                          %msup
                            %mi x
                            %mn 2
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                          %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} +
                          %msub
                            %mi H
                            %mn 1
                          %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                          %mi d
                          %msup
                            %mi x
                            %mn 1
                          %mo ∧
                          %mi d
                          %mi t
                          %mo +
                          %msub
                            %mi H
                            %mn 2
                          %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                          %mi d
                          %msup
                            %mi x
                            %mn 2
                          %mo ∧
                          %mi d
                          %mi t
                          %mo +
                          %msub
                            %mi H
                            %mn 3
                          %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                          %mi d
                          %msup
                            %mi x
                            %mn 3
                          %mo ∧
                          %mi d
                          %mi t
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\begin{aligned}
                  \\star F
                  \&amp;= -D + H\wedge dt \\
                  \&amp;= -D_1 \; d x^2 \wedge d x^3
                  \-D_2 \; d x^3 \wedge d x^1
                  \-D_3 \; d x^1 \wedge d x^2
                  \\\
                  \&amp; +
                  H_1 \; d x^1 \wedge d t
                  +
                  H_2 \; d x^2 \wedge d t
                  +
                  H_3 \; d x^3 \wedge d t
                  \\end{aligned}
          %p
            then in terms of these components the field equations – called
            %strong Maxwell’s equations
            – read as follows.
          %p
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi d
                  %mi F
                  %mo =
                  %mn 0
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} d F = 0
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %strong> magnetic Gauss law
                \:
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi div
                      %mi B
                      %mo =
                      %mn 0
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} div B = 0
            %li
              %p
                %strong> Faraday’s law
                \:
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mfrac
                        %mi d
                        %mrow
                          %mi d
                          %mi t
                      %mi B
                      %mo +
                      %mi rot
                      %mi E
                      %mo =
                      %mn 0
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \frac{d}{d t} B + rot E = 0
          %p
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi d
                  %mo ⋆
                  %mi F
                  %mo =
                  %mn 0
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} d \star F = 0
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %strong> Gauss’ law
                \:
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi div
                      %mi D
                      %mo =
                      %mi ρ
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} div D = \rho
            %li
              %p
                %strong Ampère’s law
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                      %mfrac
                        %mi d
                        %mrow
                          %mi d
                          %mi t
                      %mi D
                      %mo +
                      %mi rot
                      %mi H
                      %mo =
                      %msub
                        %mi j
                        %mi el
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} - \frac{d}{d t} D + rot H = j_{el}
          %h4#vector_potential__as_a_cechdeligne_2cocycle Vector potential – as a Cech-Deligne 2-cocycle
          %p
            By the
            = succeed "," do
              %a{:href => "#FieldStrengthAsClosed2Form"} above
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/field+strength"} field strength
            (in the absence of net
            = succeed ")" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/magnetic+charge"} magnetic charge
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/spacetime"} spacetime
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            is given by a 2-form
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi F
                  %mo ∈
                  %msup
                    %mi Ω
                    %mn 2
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi X
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F \in \Omega^2(X)
            and an
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/electric+current"} electric current
            3-form
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi j
                    %mi el
                  %mo ∈
                  %msup
                    %mi Ω
                    %mn 3
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi X
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} j_{el} \in \Omega^3(X)
            satisfying Maxwell’s equations
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi d
                  %mi F
                  %mo =
                  %mn 0
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  d F = 0
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi d
                  %mo ⋆
                  %mi F
                  %mo =
                  %msub
                    %mi j
                    %mi el
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  d \star F = j_{el}
          %p
            The first equation with the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Poincare+lemma"} Poincare lemma
            implies that one may find
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                on a
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/good+open+cover"} good open cover
                of
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi X
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
                by
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/open+subsets"} open subsets
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                      %msub
                        %mi U
                        %mi i
                      %mo →
                      %mi X
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \{U_i \to X\}
            %li
              %p
                a collection
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %msub
                        %mi A
                        %mi i
                      %mo ∈
                      %msup
                        %mi Ω
                        %mn 1
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi U
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %msub
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %mi i
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (A_i \in \Omega^1(U))_i
                of
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/differential+form"} differential 1-forms
              .maruku-equation
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi d
                      %msub
                        %mi A
                        %mi i
                      %mo =
                      %mi F
                      %msub
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                        %mrow
                          %msub
                            %mi U
                            %mi i
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                      d A_i = F|_{U_i}
            %li
              %p
                and a collection
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %msub
                        %mi λ
                        %mrow
                          %mi i
                          %mi j
                      %mo ∈
                      %msup
                        %mi C
                        %mn ∞
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %msub
                        %mi U
                        %mi i
                      %mo ∩
                      %msub
                        %mi U
                        %mi j
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo ,
                      %mi ℝ
                      %msub
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %mrow
                          %mi i
                          %mo ,
                          %mi j
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (\lambda_{i j} \in C^\infty(U_i \cap U_j), \mathbb{R})_{i,j}
                of real valued functions on double overlaps such that
              .maruku-equation
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi A
                        %mi j
                      %msub
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                        %mrow
                          %msub
                            %mi U
                            %mi i
                          %mo ∩
                          %msub
                            %mi U
                            %mi j
                      %mo −
                      %msub
                        %mi A
                        %mi i
                      %msub
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                        %mrow
                          %msub
                            %mi U
                            %mi i
                          %mo ∩
                          %msub
                            %mi U
                            %mi j
                      %mo =
                      %mi d
                      %msub
                        %mi λ
                        %mrow
                          %mi i
                          %mi j
                      %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                      %mo .
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                      A_j|_{U_i \cap U_j} -  A_i|_{U_i \cap U_j} = d \lambda_{i j}
                      \\,.
          %p
            The forms
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                  %msub
                    %mi A
                    %mi i
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \{A_i\}
            are called a
            %strong vector potential
            or the
            %strong
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/electromagnetic+potential"} electromagnetic potential
            for the electromagnetic field.
          %p
            Notice that it follows that on triple overlaps
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi U
                    %mi i
                  %mo ∩
                  %msub
                    %mi U
                    %mi j
                  %mo ∩
                  %msub
                    %mi U
                    %mi k
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} U_i \cap U_j \cap U_k
            we have
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi d
                  %msub
                    %mi λ
                    %mrow
                      %mi i
                      %mi j
                  %mo +
                  %mi d
                  %msub
                    %mi λ
                    %mrow
                      %mi j
                      %mi k
                  %mo =
                  %mi d
                  %msub
                    %mi λ
                    %mrow
                      %mi i
                      %mi k
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  d \lambda_{i j} + d \lambda_{j k} = d \lambda_{i k}
          %p which means that on that overlap the function
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi λ
                    %mrow
                      %mi i
                      %mi j
                  %mo +
                  %msub
                    %mi λ
                    %mrow
                      %mi k
                      %mi j
                  %mo −
                  %msub
                    %mi λ
                    %mrow
                      %mi i
                      %mi k
                  %mo :
                  %msub
                    %mi U
                    %mi i
                  %mo ∩
                  %msub
                    %mi U
                    %mi j
                  %mo ∩
                  %msub
                    %mi U
                    %mi k
                  %mo →
                  %mi ℝ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\lambda_{i j} + \lambda_{k j} - \lambda_{i k}
                  \:
                  U_i \cap U_j \cap U_k \to \mathbb{R}
          %p
            is constant. If one requires these constants all to be inside a discrete subgroup
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Γ
                    %mo ↪
                    %mi ℝ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Gamma \hookrightarrow \mathbb{R}
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                  %msub
                    %mi A
                    %mi i
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                  %mo ,
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                  %msub
                    %mi λ
                    %mrow
                      %mi i
                      %mi j
                  %mi mod
                  %mi Γ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (\{A_i\}, \{\lambda_{i j} mod \Gamma\})
            defines a degree 2-
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cocycle"} cocycle
            in
            = succeed "-" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Cech+cohomology"} Cech
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Deligne+cohomology"} Deligne cohomology
            on
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            with coefficients in
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ℝ
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                    %mi Γ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{R}/\Gamma
            %a{:href => "#AsBackgroundGaugeField"} Below
            we see that experiment demands that such a subgroup exists and is given by the additive group of
            = succeed "s." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/integer"} integer
          %h3#kirchhoffs_laws Kirchhoff’s laws
          %p
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Kirchhoff%27s+laws"} Kirchhoff's laws
            are a kind of coarse graining of Maxwell’s equations, where instead of infinitesimal quantities one considers actual macroscopic
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/current"} current
            and
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/voltage"} voltage
          %h3#AsBackgroundGaugeField Background gauge field for charge quantum
          %p
            The exponentiated
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/action+functional"} action functional
            for the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/sigma+model"} sigma model
            describing a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/particle"} particle
            on
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/target+space"} target space
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            charged under an electromagnetic
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/background+gauge+field"} background gauge field
            has to satisfy two properties:
          %ol
            %li
              %p
                It must be given by the
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/holonomy"} holonomy
                of the 1-forms
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi A
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
                (this encodes, via
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/variational+calculus"} variational calculus
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Lorentz+force"} Lorentz force
                exerted by the electromagnetic field on the particle ):
              .maruku-equation
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi exp
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi i
                      %mi S
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo :
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %msup
                        %mi S
                        %mn 1
                      %mover
                        %mo →
                        %mi γ
                      %mi X
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo ↦
                      %mi exp
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi i
                      %msub
                        %mi S
                        %mi kin
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi γ
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mi hol
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi γ
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                      \\exp(i S(-)) :
                      (S^1 \stackrel{\gamma}{\to} X)
                      \\mapsto
                      \\exp(i S_{kin}(\gamma)) hol(\gamma)
            %li
              %p
                It must take values in the
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/circle+group"} circle group
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi U
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mn 1
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} U(1)
                (this is a basic rule of the
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+mechanics"} quantum mechanics
                describing the particle):
              .maruku-equation
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi exp
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi i
                      %mi S
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo :
                      %msup
                        %mi C
                        %mn ∞
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %msup
                        %mi S
                        %mn 1
                      %mo ,
                      %mi X
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo →
                      %mi U
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mn 1
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                      %mo .
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                      \\exp( i S ) : C^\infty(S^1, X) \to U(1)
                      \\,.
          %p
            Therefore the above data is subject to the additional constraint that it induces well-defined
            = succeed "-valued" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi U
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} U(1)
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/holonomy"} holonomy
            – this is
            = succeed "," do
              %strong Dirac’s quantization condition
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            that are charged under the background electromagnetic field.
          %p
            Concretely: for any smooth curve
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi γ
                  %mo :
                  %msup
                    %mi S
                    %mn 1
                  %mo →
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \gamma : S^1 \to X
            and any
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cover"} cover
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                  %msub
                    %mi V
                    %mi i
                  %mo →
                  %msup
                    %mi S
                    %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \{V_i \to S^1\}
            of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi S
                    %mn 1
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S^1
            refining the pullback of the cover
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi U
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} U
            to
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi S
                      %mn 1
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S^1
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                  %mi v
                  %mo ,
                  %mi e
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \{v, e\}
            of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi S
                    %mn 1
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S^1
            subordinate to
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                    %msub
                      %mi U
                      %mi i
                    %mo →
                    %mi X
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \{U_i \to X\}
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ρ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \rho
            such that
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi γ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi e
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ⊂
                  %msub
                    %mi U
                    %mrow
                      %mi ρ
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi e
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \gamma(e) \subset U_{\rho(e)}
            and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi γ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi v
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ⊂
                  %msub
                    %mi U
                    %mrow
                      %mi ρ
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi v
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \gamma(v) \subset U_{\rho(v)}
          %p the expression
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi hol
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi γ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ≔
                  %munder
                    %mo{:lspace => "thinmathspace", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} ∏
                    %mi v
                  %mi exp
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi i
                  %msub
                    %mo ∫
                    %mi v
                  %msup
                    %mi γ
                    %mo *
                  %msub
                    %mi A
                    %mrow
                      %mi ρ
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi v
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %munder
                    %mo{:lspace => "thinmathspace", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} ∏
                    %mi e
                  %mi exp
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi i
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                  %mn 1
                  %msup
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi σ
                        %mrow
                          %mi e
                          %mo ,
                          %mi v
                  %msup
                    %mi γ
                    %mo *
                  %msub
                    %mi λ
                    %mrow
                      %mi ρ
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi e
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mi ρ
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi v
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi v
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  hol(\gamma)
                  \\coloneqq
                  \\prod_v \exp(i \int_{v} \gamma^* A_{\rho(v)})
                  \\prod_{e} \exp(i (-1)^{\sigma_{e,v}}
                  \\gamma^*\lambda_{\rho(e) \rho(v)}
                  (v)
          %p
            (where
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi σ
                    %mrow
                      %mi e
                      %mo ,
                      %mi v
                  %mo =
                  %mn 1
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \sigma_{e,v} = 1
            if
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi v
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} v
            is the final vertex of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi e
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} e
            and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                  %mn 1
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} -1
            otherwise)
          %p
            has to be a well defined element in
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi U
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} U(1)
            (independent of all the choices made).
          %p
            This implies in particular that cancelling from the triangulation an edge
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi e
                    %mi i
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} e_i
            of vanishing length must have no effect on the formula, which in turn means that for all
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi i
                  %mo ,
                  %mi j
                  %mo ,
                  %mi k
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} i,j,k
            we have
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi exp
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi i
                  %msup
                    %mi γ
                    %mo *
                  %msub
                    %mi λ
                    %mrow
                      %mi i
                      %mi j
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msub
                    %mi v
                    %mrow
                      %mi i
                      %mi j
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mi exp
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi i
                  %msup
                    %mi γ
                    %mo *
                  %msub
                    %mi λ
                    %mrow
                      %mi j
                      %mi k
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msub
                    %mi v
                    %mrow
                      %mi j
                      %mi k
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo =
                  %mi exp
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi i
                  %msup
                    %mi γ
                    %mo *
                  %msub
                    %mi λ
                    %mrow
                      %mi i
                      %mi k
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msub
                    %mi v
                    %mrow
                      %mi i
                      %mi k
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\exp(i \gamma^*\lambda_{i j}(v_{i j}))
                  \\exp(i \gamma^*\lambda_{j k}(v_{j k}))
                  \=
                  \\exp(i \gamma^*\lambda_{i k}(v_{i k}))
          %p and hence
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi λ
                    %mrow
                      %mi i
                      %mi j
                  %mo +
                  %msub
                    %mi λ
                    %mrow
                      %mi j
                      %mi k
                  %mo =
                  %msub
                    %mi λ
                    %mrow
                      %mi i
                      %mi k
                  %mi mod
                  %mn 2
                  %mi π
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\lambda_{i j} + \lambda_{j k} =
                  \\lambda_{i k} mod 2\pi
                  \\,.
          %p
            In short: the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/holonomy"} holonomy
            of the constant path on a point
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi x
                  %mo ∈
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x \in X
            must be
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mn 1
                    %mo ∈
                    %mi U
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 1 \in U(1)
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi U
                    %mi i
                  %mo ∩
                  %msub
                    %mi U
                    %mi j
                  %mo ∩
                  %msub
                    %mi U
                    %mi k
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} U_i \cap U_j \cap U_k
            then the holonomy is equivalently given as the exponentiated sum of the three transition functions. This forces the sum
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi λ
                    %mrow
                      %mi i
                      %mi j
                  %mo +
                  %msub
                    %mi λ
                    %mrow
                      %mi j
                      %mi k
                  %mo −
                  %msub
                    %mi λ
                    %mrow
                      %mi i
                      %mi k
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \lambda_{i j} + \lambda_{j k} - \lambda_{i k}
            to land in
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ℤ
                    %mo ↪
                    %mi ℝ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{Z} \hookrightarrow \mathbb{R}
          %p In total this says precisely that the data
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msub
                    %mi A
                    %mi i
                  %mo ,
                  %msub
                    %mi λ
                    %mrow
                      %mi i
                      %mi j
                  %mi mod
                  %mi ℤ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  (A_i, \lambda_{i j} mod \mathbb{Z})
          %p
            is a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%C4%8Cech+cohomology"} Čech cocycle
            with coefficients in the degree 2
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Deligne+cohomology"} Deligne complex
            whose
            = succeed "2-form" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/curvature"} curvature
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/curvature"} curvature
            2-form.
          %h2#ChargeQuantization Charge quantization
          %p
            See at
            = succeed "." do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Dirac+charge+quantization"} Dirac charge quantization
          %center
            %img{:src => "https://ncatlab.org/nlab/files/DiracChargeQuantizationII.jpg", :width => "640"}/
          %h3#DiracArgument Dirac’s original argument
          %blockquote
            %p under construction
          %p Dirac originally presented the following reasoning, which captures the main point of the above considerations.
          %p
            He considered
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            to be
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi ℝ
                    %mn 3
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{R}^3
            without the origin,
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                  %mo ≔
                  %msup
                    %mi ℝ
                    %mn 3
                  %mo \
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                  %mn 0
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo ,
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  X \coloneqq \mathbb{R}^3 \backslash \{0\}
                  \\,,
          %p
            which is a manifold of the topology of (
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/weak+homotopy+equivalence"}> weakly homotopy equivalent to
            ) the 2-
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/sphere"} sphere
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi S
                      %mn 2
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S^2
          %p
            He imagined a situation with a magnetic charge supported on the point located at the origin and removed that point in order to keep the field strength
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi F
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F
            to be a
            %em closed
            2-form on all of
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
          %p
            (Indeed, if one does not remove the support of magnetic charge, the argument becomes much more sophisticated and involves higher differential cocycles given by
            = succeed "s." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/bundle+gerbe"} bundle gerbe
            = succeed ".)" do
              %a{:href => "http://arxiv.org/abs/hep-th/0011220"} Dirac charge quantization and generalized differential cohomology
          %p Then he considered a single coordinate patch
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi U
                  %mo ≔
                  %msup
                    %mi ℝ
                    %mn 3
                  %mo \
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                  %msup
                    %mi x
                    %mn 1
                  %mo ≥
                  %mn 0
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                  %mo ⊂
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  U \coloneqq \mathbb{R}^3 \backslash \{x^1 \geq 0\} \subset X
          %p
            given by
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            minus the right half of the first coordinate axis.
          %p Traditionally physicists try to give that half-line a physical interpretation by imagining that it is the body of an idealized infinitely-thin and to one side infinitely-long solenoid. Indeed, such a solenoid would have a magnetic monopole charge on each of its ends, so if the one end is imagined to have disappeared to infinity, then the other one is the magnetic charge that Dirac imagines to sit at the origin of our setup.
          %p
            In this context the half-line
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                  %msup
                    %mi x
                    %mn 1
                  %mo ≥
                  %mn 0
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \{x^1 \geq 0\}
            is called a
            = succeed "." do
              %strong Dirac string
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/fiber+bundles"} fiber bundles
            (see at
            = succeed ")." do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/fiber+bundles+in+physics"} fiber bundles in physics
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi U
                  %mo ≔
                  %msup
                    %mi ℝ
                    %mn 3
                  %mo \
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                  %msup
                    %mi x
                    %mn 1
                  %mo ≥
                  %mn 0
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                  %mo ⊂
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} U \coloneqq \mathbb{R}^3 \backslash \{x^1 \geq 0\} \subset X
            as exactly what it is, by itself: an open subset that is part of a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cover"} cover
            of
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cover"} cover
            (at least one more is needed for a decent discussion), so that the Dirac string keeps haunting physicists.
          %blockquote
            %p …running out of time…just quickly now…
          %p
            …Dirac effectively considered the overlap cocycle condition
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi A
                      %mi j
                    %mo −
                    %msub
                      %mi A
                      %mi i
                    %mo =
                    %mi something
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A_j - A_i = something
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi A
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
            has well defined holonomy it follows that there must be
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi g
                    %mi ij
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} g_{ij}
            a function with values in
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi U
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} U(1)
            such that
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi A
                      %mi j
                    %mo −
                    %msub
                      %mi A
                      %mi i
                    %mo =
                    %mi d
                    %mi log
                    %msub
                      %mi g
                      %mi ij
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A_j - A_i = d log g_{ij}
            = succeed "-patch" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi j
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} j
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi A
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
            must be the log-differential of a
            = succeed "-valued" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi U
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} U(1)
          %blockquote
            %p …have to run…
          %p In modern terms:
          %p
            The
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/clutching+construction"} clutching construction
            gives
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/principal+U%281%29-bundle"} principal U(1)-bundle
            ob
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi S
                    %mn 2
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S^2
            by covering with two hemispheres
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi U
                    %mn 0
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} U_0
            and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi U
                    %mn 1
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} U_1
            and picking a transition function
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi g
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                  %msup
                    %mi S
                    %mn 1
                  %mo →
                  %mi U
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} g \colon S^1 \to U(1)
            on the overlap
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi U
                      %mn 0
                    %mo ∩
                    %msub
                      %mi U
                      %mn 1
                    %mo ≃
                    %msup
                      %mi S
                      %mn 1
                    %mo ×
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mn 0
                    %mo ,
                    %mi ϵ
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} U_0 \cap U_1 \simeq S^1\times (0,\epsilon)
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/winding+number"} winding number
            of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi g
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} g
            represents the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/first+Chern+class"} first Chern class
            of the line bundle.
          %p
            By
            %a{:href => "connection+on+a+bundle#ExistenceOfConnections"} the standard formula
            for existence of principal connections on given principal bundles, given a choice of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/partition+of+unity"} partition of unity
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                  %msub
                    %mi ρ
                    %mi i
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \{\rho_i\}
            then the connection on
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi U
                    %mn 0
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} U_0
            is given by
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi A
                    %mn 0
                  %mo =
                  %msub
                    %mi ρ
                    %mn 1
                  %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                    %mi d
                  %mi log
                  %mi g
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  A_0 = \rho_1 \mathbf{d} log g
          %p
            If we think (as we may) of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi U
                    %mn 0
                  %mo =
                  %msup
                    %mi S
                    %mn 2
                  %mo −
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                  %mo *
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} U_0 = S^2 - \{\ast\}
            as covering most of the 2-sphere except one point and of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi U
                    %mn 1
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} U_1
            the
            = succeed "-open" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ϵ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \epsilon
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi A
                    %mn 0
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A_0
            vanishes on most of the sphere and close to the point taken out (“the Dirac string”) it becomes non-vanishing and equal to
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi g
                      %mrow
                        %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                        %mn 1
                    %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                      %mi d
                    %mi g
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} g^{-1}\mathbf{d}g
          %h3#electricmagnetic_charge_quantization Electric-magnetic charge quantization
          %p
            Suppose we had a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/circle+bundle"} circle bundle
            with
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/connection+on+a+bundle"} connection on a bundle
            representing an electromagnetic field with net
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/magnetic+charge"} magnetic charge
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Q
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Q
            given by some magnetic
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/current"} current
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi j
                    %mi mag
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} j_{mag}
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi d
                  %mi F
                  %mo =
                  %msub
                    %mi j
                    %mi mag
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  d F = j_{mag}
          %p
            that is supported in some compact spatial region
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi U
                  %mo ×
                  %mi ℝ
                  %mo ⊂
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} U \times \mathbb{R} \subset X
            with boundary sphere
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo ∂
                    %mi U
                    %mo ≃
                    %msup
                      %mi S
                      %mn 2
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \partial U \simeq S^2
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi q
                  %mo =
                  %msub
                    %mo ∫
                    %mi U
                  %msub
                    %mi j
                    %mi mag
                  %mo =
                  %msub
                    %mo ∫
                    %mi U
                  %mi dF
                  %mo =
                  %msub
                    %mo ∫
                    %mrow
                      %mo ∂
                      %mi U
                  %mi F
                  %mo =
                  %msub
                    %mo ∫
                    %mrow
                      %msup
                        %mi S
                        %mn 2
                  %mi F
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  q = \int_U j_{mag} = \int_U dF =\int_{\partial U} F = \int_{S^2} F
          %p
            by the
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Stokes+theorem"} Stokes theorem
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi F
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F
            is the curvature 2-form on a circle bundle that
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi q
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} q
            is integral: it is given by the first
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Chern+class"} Chern class
            of the bundle.
          %p (…)
          %p
            For
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi γ
                  %mo :
                  %msup
                    %mi S
                    %mn 1
                  %mo →
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \gamma : S^1 \to X
            a closed but contractible trajectory of an electrically charged particle, the action functional is
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi exp
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi i
                  %mi e
                  %msub
                    %mo ∫
                    %mrow
                      %msup
                        %mi S
                        %mn 1
                  %msup
                    %mi γ
                    %mo *
                  %mi A
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo =
                  %mi exp
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi i
                  %mi e
                  %msub
                    %mo ∫
                    %mrow
                      %msup
                        %mi D
                        %mn 2
                  %msup
                    %mi γ
                    %mo *
                  %mi F
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\exp(i e \int_{S^1} \gamma^* A )
                  \=
                  \\exp(i e \int_{D^2} \gamma^* F)
          %p
            by the
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Stokes+theorem"} Stokes theorem
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi γ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \gamma
            approaches a constant path and the 2-disk is taken to wrap the 2-cycle
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo ∂
                    %mi U
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \partial U
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mn 1
                  %mo =
                  %mi exp
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi i
                  %mi e
                  %msub
                    %mo ∫
                    %mrow
                      %msup
                        %mi S
                        %mn 2
                  %mi F
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo =
                  %mi exp
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi i
                  %mi e
                  %mi q
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  1 = \exp(i e \int_{S^2} F) = \exp(i e q)
                  \\,.
          %p Which implies that with the magnetic charge being quantized, also the electric charge is.
          %p (…)
          %h2#related_concepts Related concepts
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/voltage"} voltage
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/fiber+bundles+in+physics"} fiber bundles in physics
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Dirac+charge+quantization"} Dirac charge quantization
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/dipole+moment"} dipole moment
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Hodge-Maxwell+theorem"} Hodge-Maxwell theorem
            %li
              %p
